CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ int swappable: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ probe checks the declaration only, not the definition: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
__is_swappable-style SFINAE probe (two-overload __declval chain + decltype
default template argument), read through a class-local typedef by an in-class
static data member initializer.  The root cause turned out NOT to be
[temp.inst]/5 definition-instantiation from the unevaluated operand: bisection
showed `static const bool value = type::value;` reading ANY class-local
typedef mis-evaluated, because the in-class initializer was type-checked
mid-elaboration (sibling members unresolvable) and, on failure, silently left
as a raw cpp_name that reads as nondet.  Fixed per N5008
[basic.scope.class] (the initializer is in the scope of the class, so
earlier-declared member names must resolve): initializers whose value
still contains a cpp_name are queued and re-type-checked in the class scope
once the class is complete.  g++/clang++ runtime-verified.
